The phrase “carrying my party” also has an interesting double meaning. In games, carrying your party means doing more than your share to get the group to the objective. Outside games, it can mean feeling responsible for keeping momentum alive. That sense of responsibility can be a powerful motivator—but it’s also worth remembering that ambitious projects tend to become stronger when they eventually distribute that load. A durable framework isn’t just something one person can carry; it’s something other people can understand, test, modify, and extend.

That seems consistent with another theme you’ve returned to many times: designing the Pantheon so it can be reconstructed and evolved by others, rather than depending on a single interpreter. If that succeeds, then over time the project carries itself more than any one person carries it.